{
  "primary_meaning": "The end of the age / final judgment, when God reaps the earth, gathers the righteous (as wheat or grain) into his kingdom, and separates or destroys the wicked",
  "primary_basis": [
    "Matthew 13:24-30, 36-43 (parable of the weeds: 'the harvest is the end of the age, and the reapers are angels')",
    "Revelation 14:14-20 (the harvest of the earth with the sickle of the Son of Man)",
    "Joel 3:13 ('Put in the sickle, for the harvest is ripe')",
    "Matthew 3:12 / Luke 3:17 (winnowing and burning of chaff)"
  ],
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "meaning": "The present mission field / ready souls awaiting evangelism and gathering into the church",
      "basis": [
        "Matthew 9:37-38 / Luke 10:2 ('The harvest is plentiful, but the laborers are few')",
        "John 4:35 ('the fields are white for harvest')"
      ],
      "prominence": "common"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "The reaping of moral or spiritual consequences / fruit of one's deeds or ministry",
      "basis": [
        "Galatians 6:7-9 ('whatever one sows, that will he also reap')",
        "2 Corinthians 9:6",
        "Hosea 10:12-13"
      ],
      "prominence": "common"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "God's material or spiritual provision and blessing (abundance as sign of divine favor)",
      "basis": [
        "Old Testament harvest festivals and promises (e.g., Deuteronomy 16; Psalm 67:6)",
        "Parables of growth and fruitfulness"
      ],
      "prominence": "minority"
    }
  ],
  "has_settled_consensus": true,
  "confidence": "high",
  "notes": "The eschatological-judgment sense is the most consistently emphasized symbolic reading in biblical studies and historic Christian teaching when 'harvest' functions as a metaphor; contextual uses for evangelism and moral reaping are also frequent but secondary or complementary rather than competing primaries. No single meaning is exclusive; usage is context-dependent across OT and NT."
}
